				V // THE TOOL-RELAY CALLBACK
			</div>
			<div
				class="zine-box-canary w-full border-4 border-blood-crimson bg-carbon-void p-8 transition-transform duration-300 hover:rotate-0 sm:-rotate-[0.5deg] sm:p-12"
			>
				<div class="grid grid-cols-1 gap-32 lg:grid-cols-2">
					<div class="flex flex-col gap-12">
						<h2
							class="font-headline text-2xl font-black uppercase tracking-tight text-bone-white sm:text-4xl"
						>
							Synchronous, unless you say otherwise
						</h2>
						<p class="font-body text-sm leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
							<code class="font-mono text-xs">POST /tool/relay</code> runs the tool
							synchronously unless the request carries a
							<code class="font-mono text-xs">callback_url</code> - then the call
							becomes asynchronous: the proxy answers immediately with
							<code class="font-mono text-xs">{"{success: true, async_call: true}"}</code>,
							executes the tool, and POSTs the real result to the callback URL later
							(docs/tool-relay/callbacks.md:29-48).
						</p>
						<p class="font-body text-sm leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
							The guardrails are strict. The callback URL must be
							<strong class="text-bone-white">https-only</strong> - http, including
							loopback, and any other scheme are rejected with HTTP 400 before
							anything executes (SSRF protection). The delivery is a
							<strong class="text-bone-white">5-second, fire-and-forget POST</strong>:
							no auth header, no retries, the outcome untracked (only relay calls
							themselves are counted in
							<code class="font-mono text-xs">/stats</code>). Only
							<code class="font-mono text-xs">aphrodite_retrieve</code>,
							<code class="font-mono text-xs">aphrodite_compress</code> and
							<code class="font-mono text-xs">aphrodite_list</code> are relayable;
							anything else is an error. On graceful shutdown the task tracker waits
							for in-flight deliveries, so none are lost mid-flight.
						</p>
					</div>
					<div class="flex flex-col gap-8">
						<div class="font-mono text-[10px] font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-zinc-dust">
							THE TWO LANES OF ONE RELAY CALL
						</div>
						<div class="border border-oxblood-dark bg-soot-black p-5 font-mono text-[12px]">
							<div class="text-zinc-dust">SYNCHRONOUS // NO CALLBACK_URL</div>
							<div class="mt-4 text-bone-newsprint opacity-90">POST /tool/relay - tool runs - full result inline</div>
							<div class="mt-8 border-t border-oxblood-dark pt-4 text-[10px] text-stamped-canary">
								ASYNC // WITH CALLBACK_URL
							</div>
							<div class="mt-4 text-bone-newsprint opacity-90">
								{"{ \"success\": true, \"result\": null, \"async_call\": true }"}
							</div>
							<div class="mt-2 text-zinc-dust">... later, 5s timeout, no auth, no retries ...</div>
							<div class="mt-2 text-bone-newsprint opacity-90">POST callback_url - the real result</div>
						</div>
						<div class="border border-oxblood-dark bg-asphalt-panel p-5 font-body text-[13px] leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
							<span class="font-mono text-[10px] font-bold uppercase tracking-widest text-raw-blood">THE RESULT ITSELF · </span>
							is compact metadata (hash, type, size, marker) - so it stays raw through
							the hook pipeline: compressing an
							<code class="font-mono text-xs">aphrodite_retrieve</code> result would
							replace resolved content with another marker.
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>
				<figure class="mt-24 border border-oxblood-dark bg-asphalt-panel p-4" data-diagram="tool-relay">
					<div class="overflow-x-auto" set:html={ToolRelay} />
					<figcaption class="mt-6 font-mono text-[10px] font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-zinc-dust">
						THE RELAY // VALIDATE HTTPS - EXECUTE - SPAWN THE 5S CALLBACK
					</figcaption>
				</figure>
