							A single binary can run two listeners with different roles - the cache
							proxy on <code class="font-mono text-xs">:9797</code> (in-memory
							DashMap, 10,000 entries, 8192-byte threshold) and the token proxy on
							<code class="font-mono text-xs">:9798</code> (SQLite at
							<code class="font-mono text-xs">ccr.db</code>, 1024-byte threshold,
							persistent, tool-relay-capable). Each has its own
							<code class="font-mono text-xs">AppState</code> and CCR backend; both
							bind to loopback by default (docs/proxy/architecture.md:9-35).
						</p>
						<p class="font-body text-sm leading-relaxed text-bone-newsprint">
							The parallel that matters: the same payload crosses the plugin-hook lane
							(intercepted directly at the tool boundary, no API round-trip) and the
							proxy lane (Chat Completions responses compressed in flight) - and the
							marker it becomes is byte-identical across the two paths, as are the
							enriched previews (CHANGELOG.md:616-617). The preview-doubling
							regression of v1.3.4 was caught precisely because the two paths were
							held to that standard.
						</p>
